DR  Complex Portal; CPX-6036;
DR  Complex Portal; CPX-1831;
CC  Eukaryotic translation initiation factor 3 (eIF3) is the largest of the
CC  translation initiation factors. It binds the 40S ribosomal subunit and
CC  promotes recruitment of eIF1, eIF1A, eIF5 and the eIF2-GTP-Met-tRNAi ternary
CC  complex to form the 43S pre-initiation complex, and it stimulates attachment
CC  of this complex to mRNA and scanning for the start codon [2]. It also takes
CC  part in recycling post-termination ribosomes and prevents premature joining
CC  of the 40S and 60S subunits. Mammalian eIF3 contains 13 subunits, eIF3a to
CC  eIF3m [1]. Subunits a, b, c, g and i form a conserved core, and the eIF3 of
CC  Saccharomyces cerevisiae consists of this core plus subunit j [2]. Only the
CC  five core subunits are required steps in this property; the other eight are
CC  optional.
**  Created from Complex Portal CPX-6036 (human) as a discovery-pipeline
**  candidate (discovery/candidates/complexportal). Evidence is the HAMAP rule
**  for each subunit (MF_03000 to MF_03012), checked against InterPro 110.0.
**  Required steps limited to the a/b/c/g/i core so that eIF3 of budding yeast,
**  which lacks d, e, f, h, k, l and m, is called YES. Not yet placed in the
**  hierarchy; a parent CATEGORY needs choosing. Needs curator review before
**  being made public.
